:3000 {
	# Caddy's reverse_proxy APPENDS to an existing X-Forwarded-For by
	# default, rather than replacing it. Without this override, a client
	# sending "X-Forwarded-For: 1.2.3.4" would arrive at Next as
	# "1.2.3.4, <real address>" -- the web server action forwards that
	# whole string to the API, and client_ip.resolve takes
	# forwarded.split(",")[0], i.e. the forged value. That would hand the
	# caller its own rate-limit bucket and undo the point of the repair in
	# apps/web/middleware.ts. Replacing rather than appending is what makes
	# trusting the header downstream safe. Do not remove this line.
	#
	# This has no `trusted_proxies` directive, which is correct as long as
	# Caddy is the outermost hop -- {remote_host} is then genuinely the
	# visitor's address. It stops being correct the moment something sits
	# in front of Caddy (a TLS terminator, a load balancer, a CDN): every
	# visitor would then collapse onto that edge's own address here, and
	# TICKET_IP_HOURLY_CAP throttles the whole internet to five tickets an
	# hour, silently -- the exact defect this file's override exists to fix,
	# recurring one hop further out. If you add such a hop, add a
	# `trusted_proxies` directive to the `reverse_proxy` block below naming
	# it, so Caddy derives X-Forwarded-For from a connection it actually
	# trusts instead of believing whatever arrives (see README.md, "A
	# reverse proxy in front of Next").
	reverse_proxy web:3000 {
		header_up X-Forwarded-For {remote_host}
	}
}
